  "intro": "A sequence $\\{a_n\\}$ lists terms indexed by $n=1,2,3,\\dots$. It converges to $L$ if $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n=L$ exists (a finite number), and diverges otherwise. Many limits follow the same rules as function limits: for rational expressions compare degrees, and useful facts include $\\lim r^n=0$ when $|r|<1$ and $a_n\\to L$ when the terms are squeezed between two sequences with limit $L$. A sequence is monotonic if it is always increasing or always decreasing, and bounded if its terms stay within fixed bounds.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Does $a_n=\\dfrac{2n+1}{n+3}$ converge? If so, to what?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Divide numerator and denominator by $n$: $\\frac{2+1/n}{1+3/n}$.",
          "As $n\\to\\infty$, the $1/n$ terms vanish.",
          "The limit is $\\frac{2}{1}=2$, so the sequence converges to $2$."
        ],
        "answer": "Converges to $2$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Does $a_n=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^n$ converge?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "This is $r^n$ with $r=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $|r|<1$.",
          "So $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n=0$.",
          "The sequence converges to $0$."
        ],
        "answer": "Converges to $0$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Does $a_n=(-1)^n$ converge?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "The terms alternate: $-1,1,-1,1,\\dots$.",
          "They do not approach a single value.",
          "So the sequence diverges (no limit)."
        ],
        "answer": "Diverges"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 4,
      "prompt": "Limit of $a_n=\\dfrac{3n}{n+1}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Divide by $n$: $\\frac{3}{1+1/n}\\to 3$."
        ],
        "answer": "$3$"
      }
